'''Sakata Shinmei-gū''' (坂田神明宮) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|ja|神社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinto shrine|qq=}} located in {{ill|Maibara|ja|米原市|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Maibara|qq=}}, {{ill|Shiga Prefecture|ja|滋賀県|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shiga Prefecture|qq=}}. It consists of two shrines: the '''Sakata Shrine''' (Inner Shrine) and '''Oka Shrine''' (Outer Shrine). Sakata Shrine is associated with the {{ill|Motoise|ja|元伊勢|1=Moto-Ise Shrines|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Motoise|13=User:Immanuelle/Moto-Ise Shrines|qq=}} tradition, while Oka Shrine is identified as a {{ill|Shikinaisha|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|qq=draft|1=Shikinaisha|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|qq=}}.

== Deities ==
* {{ill|Amaterasu|ja|天照皇大神|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Amaterasu|qq=}} (Sakata Shrine, Inner Shrine)
* {{ill|Toyouke-bime|ja|豊受毘売命|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Toyouke-bime|qq=}} (Oka Shrine, Outer Shrine)

== Shrine Crest ==
The shrine crest is a round emblem with a tachibana (Japanese orange).

== History ==
Sakata Shrine, historically referred to as "Sakata Daijingu," was revered as the {{ill|Sōja (Shinto)|ja|総社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sōja (Shinto)|qq=}} of {{ill|Sakata District, Ōmi Province|ja|坂田郡|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sakata District, Ōmi Province|qq=}}. According to the {{ill|Ise Jingū|ja|伊勢神宮|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ise Jingū|qq=}} chronicle, the ''{{ill|Yamatohime no Mikoto Seiki|ja|倭姫命世記|1=Yamatohime no Mikoto Seiki|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yamatohime no Mikoto Seiki|13=User:Immanuelle/Yamatohime no Mikoto Seiki|qq=}}'', {{ill|Yamatohime-no-Mikoto|ja|倭姫命|1=Yamatohime-no-mikoto|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yamatohime-no-Mikoto|13=User:Immanuelle/Yamatohime-no-mikoto|qq=}} moved the deity Amaterasu to Sakata Shrine from "Kōga no Hikumo no Miya" in the 8th year of Emperor Suinin's reign, where the deity was enshrined for two years. The shrine traces its origins to the establishment of Sakata Shrine in honor of Yamatohime-no-Mikoto's divine mission. The region later became a {{ill|Mikuriya|ja|御厨|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Mikuriya|qq=}}, or land directly controlled by the Ise Grand Shrine, and its connection to sacred rice paddies dates back to this era.<ref>''Ise no Jingū.''</ref>

Oka Shrine was historically known as "Oka Tennōsha" or "Wakamiya."<ref>{{ill|Deguchi Nobutsune|ja|出口延経|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Deguchi Nobutsune|qq=}}'s ''{{ill|Jinmyōchō Kōshō|ja|神名帳考証|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Jinmyōchō Kōshō|qq=}}''.</ref> According to tradition, during the reign of Emperor Kōan, three deities descended in the area: Ugano-tama no Mikoto (also known as Toyouke-bime), Miketsu-kami in Chikuma Village, and Ōtoshi-kami in Iwawaki Village.<ref>The Chikuma deity is currently enshrined at {{ill|Chikuma Shrine (Maibara)|ja|筑摩神社 (米原市)|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Chikuma Shrine (Maibara)|qq=}}, and the Iwawaki deity at {{ill|Inari Shrine (Maibara)|ja|稲荷神社 (米原市岩脇)}.}</ref> Oka Shrine later became identified as the "Oka Shrine" listed in the {{ill|Engishiki|ja|延喜式神名帳|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Engishiki|13=User:Immanuelle/Engishiki Jinmyōchō|qq=}}, and its name is believed to be connected to the local village name, Ugano.<ref name="郡志">''Ōmi Sakata District Gazetteer'' (大正2nd year , Sakata District Office).</ref> Over time, Oka Shrine was merged with Sakata Shrine.

During the {{ill|Kenmu Restoration|ja|建武の新政|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kenmu Restoration|qq=}}, the shrine suffered damages due to war but was reconstructed by {{ill|Sasaki Hidetsuna|ja|佐々木秀綱|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sasaki Hidetsuna|qq=}}. It was destroyed and rebuilt multiple times, including during the {{ill|Ōnin War|ja|応仁の乱|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ōnin War|qq=}}. In 1733, it was restored under the patronage of {{ill|Ii Naochika|ja|井伊直惟|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ii Naochika|qq=}}, the lord of Hikone Domain, who reorganized and expanded the shrine grounds, after which it was officially named Sakata Shinmei-gū.<ref name="郡志"/>

In modern times, the shrine was designated a {{ill|Gōsha|ja|郷社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gōsha|qq=}} on February 1, 1881, and elevated to a prefectural shrine in 1945.

The shrine grounds are bisected by the {{ill|Hokuriku Main Line|ja|北陸本線|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hokuriku Main Line|qq=}}, with a railway crossing located on the approach to the shrine. The nearest station is {{ill|Sakata Station (Shiga)|ja|坂田駅|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sakata Station (Shiga)|qq=}}. Other shrines with railway crossings within their grounds include:
* {{ill|Henshōin|ja|遍照院|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Henshōin|qq=}} (Kanagawa Prefecture, Yokohama City, served by Keikyū Main Line)
* {{ill|Shimozuhara Hachiman Shrine|ja|下原八幡神社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shimozuhara Hachiman Shrine|qq=}} (Gifu Prefecture, Gero City, served by JR Takayama Line)
* {{ill|Ishibiki Shrine|ja|石引神社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ishibiki Shrine|qq=}} (Gifu Prefecture, Ōgaki City, served by Seino Railway)
* {{ill|Seki-Semimaru Shrine|ja|関蝉丸神社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Seki-Semimaru Shrine|qq=}} (Shiga Prefecture, Ōtsu City, served by Keihan Keishin Line)
* {{ill|Sawada Hachiman Shrine|ja|澤田八幡神社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sawada Hachiman Shrine|qq=}} (Osaka Prefecture, Fujiidera City, served by Kintetsu Minami-Osaka Line)
* {{ill|Sueyama Shrine|ja|陶山神社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sueyama Shrine|qq=}} (Saga Prefecture, Arita Town, served by JR Sasebo Line)

